UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 12: The design process', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Transfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new need, not copying Jordan’s pencil answers. The process is: name the user and the problem, write a brief with constraints, set checks you can try, describe real objects you can see, sketch different structures, choose one and name a trade-off, plan cardboard and scissors with an adult nearby, separate the prototype from the final idea, test, record a fail as information, and evaluate what was met. A new user changes the facts. “Holds 8 pencils” is Jordan’s criterion. It does not belong on someone else’s brief unless that person also has that need. Copying the title and keeping every pencil sentence is not transfer.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nApply the first three process steps to a friend who loses hair ties in a sports bag. Write the user and the problem, one home-cardboard constraint, and one checkable criterion. Do not write a puzzle brief, and do not reuse “holds 8 pencils”.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe friend loses hair ties in a sports bag; one constraint is home cardboard that fits the side pocket. User: the friend. Problem: hair ties vanish in the bag. Criterion: holds 6 hair ties and they stay in when the bag is tipped gently once, with no sharp edge. That is the same process with new facts. “Holds 8 pencils” does not describe hair ties. Scissors and an adult nearby remain the making limit when a model is eventually planned.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCopying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title. The process stays, but the facts must change. A new person has a new need, new constraints and new checks. If the criterion still talks about pencils, the transfer has not happened.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a fresh brief for a younger sibling, about six, whose jigsaw pieces scatter across the floor. Include the user, the need, two constraints (zero cost and home cardboard), and two criteria that are not pencil tests. State the making limit: scissors and cardboard only, with an adult nearby. Do not reuse Jordan’s eight-pencil count, the 15 cm desk corner, or the recipe-card example.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Design and Technology","learning_intention":"Transfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new need, not copying Jordan’s pencil answers. The process is: name the user and the problem, write a brief with constraints, set checks you can try, describe real objects you can see, sketch different structures, choose one and name a trade-off, plan cardboard and scissors with an adult nearby, separate the prototype from the final idea, test, record a fail as information, and evaluate what was met. A new user changes the facts. “Holds 8 pencils” is Jordan’s criterion. It does not belong on someone else’s brief unless that person also has that need. Copying the title and keeping every pencil sentence is not transfer.","lesson_number":12,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DIT5-EVL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a fresh brief for a younger sibling, about six, whose jigsaw pieces scatter across the floor. Include the user, the need, two constraints (zero cost and home cardboard), and two criteria that are not pencil tests.  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using transfer on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title.. Correction: The friend loses hair ties in a sports bag; one constraint is home cardboard that fits the side pocket.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title. The process stays, but the facts must change. A new person has a new need, new constraints and new checks. If the criterion still talks about pencils, the transfer has not happened. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating transfer on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with transfer, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about transfer with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for transfer. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for transfer. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The friend loses hair ties in a sports bag","Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work"],"question":"Apply the first three process steps to a friend who loses hair ties in a sports bag. Write the user and the problem, one home-cardboard constraint, and one checkable criterion. Do not write a puzzle brief, and do not reuse “holds 8 pencils”. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for transfer; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The friend loses hair ties in a sports bag","Careful use of process with clear evidence","Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title","Transfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new need, not copying Jordan’s pencil answers"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named process, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title","Name process only, without linking it to transfer","Apply transfer to new material and name the process used"],"question":"Which response best applies transfer with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on transfer. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title","The friend loses hair ties in a sports bag","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves transfer","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Transfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","A grandparent’s recipe cards slide off the bench"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name process only, with no linked example","Write a fresh brief for a younger sibling, about six, whose jigsaw pieces scatter across the floor","Retype the worked example with no new example","Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title"],"question":"Which independent response best applies transfer to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about transfer with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating transfer on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using transfer on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for transfer. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with transfer, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title.. Correction: The friend loses hair ties in a sports bag; one constraint is home cardboard that fits the side pocket.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-design-technology-y9-w06-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Catalogue subject is Design and Technology. Proposed Year 7 alignment uses Design Innovation Technology 7–10 (2025) outcome DIT5-EVL-01, which the syllabus says may be adjusted for Stage 4 (DIT4-ADJ-01). The 2019 Design and Technology syllabus is in teach-out until 2029. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Practical work is paper or cardboard only.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise transfer.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Design and Technology","success_criteria":["I can use transfer with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify process and explain why it matters.","I can practise sibling independently on a new prompt.","I can notice puzzle needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new need, not copying Jordan’s pencil answers.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Transfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new need, not copying Jordan’s pencil answers","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA grandparent’s recipe cards slide off the bench; the process moves to that new user without pencil criteria. User and problem: a grandparent, recipe cards slide off the kitchen bench while cooking. Constraints: zero cost, sits beside the toaster, paper or cardboard from home. One checkable criterion: holds 10 cards and they stay put when the bench is wiped once, with no sharp edge. This worked transfer is not Jordan’s organiser and it is not a puzzle-piece brief. Safety stays the same: scissors only, adult nearby, no power tool.\n\nTRY FIRST\nApply the first three process steps to a friend who loses hair ties in a sports bag. Write the user and the problem, one home-cardboard constraint, and one checkable criterion. Do not write a puzzle brief, and do not reuse “holds 8 pencils”.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe friend loses hair ties in a sports bag; one constraint is home cardboard that fits the side pocket. User: the friend. Problem: hair ties vanish in the bag. Criterion: holds 6 hair ties and they stay in when the bag is tipped gently once, with no sharp edge. That is the same process with new facts. “Holds 8 pencils” does not describe hair ties. Scissors and an adult nearby remain the making limit when a model is eventually planned.","explain":"Transfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new need, not copying Jordan’s pencil answers. The process is: name the user and the problem, write a brief with constraints, set checks you can try, describe real objects you can see, sketch different structures, choose one and name a trade-off, plan cardboard and scissors with an adult nearby, separate the prototype from the final idea, test, record a fail as information, and evaluate what was met. A new user changes the facts. “Holds 8 pencils” is Jordan’s criterion. It does not belong on someone else’s brief unless that person also has that need. Copying the title and keeping every pencil sentence is not transfer.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title. The process stays, but the facts must change. A new person has a new need, new constraints and new checks. If the criterion still talks about pencils, the transfer has not happened. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"The design-process steps beside a reminder that a new user needs new facts.","caption":"Transfer keeps the process and changes the user, need and criteria.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Same process</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">User, limits, check, test</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">New facts</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Not the pencil answers</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A grandparent’s recipe cards slide off the bench; the process moves to that new user without pencil criteria. User and p Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A grandparent’s recipe cards slide off the bench; the process moves to that new user without pencil criteria. User and p Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA grandparent’s recipe cards slide off the bench; the process moves to that new user without pencil criteria. User and problem: a grandparent, recipe cards slide off the kitchen bench while cooking. Constraints: zero cost, sits beside the toaster, paper or cardboard from home. One checkable criterion: holds 10 cards and they stay put when the bench is wiped once, with no sharp edge. This worked transfer is not Jordan’s organiser and it is not a puzzle-piece brief. Safety stays the same: scissors only, adult nearby, no power tool. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"A recipe-card worked example beside the separate sibling puzzle-piece brief.","caption":"The independent brief uses a younger sibling, not Jordan’s pencils.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Recipe-card model</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">10 cards stay when wiped</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sibling task</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Puzzle pieces on the floor</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCopying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title. The process stays, but the facts must change. A new person has a new need, new constraints and new checks. If the criterion still talks about pencils, the transfer has not happened. Here is a more productive model: A grandparent’s recipe cards slide off the bench; the process moves to that new user without pencil criteria. User and problem: a grandparent, recipe cards slide off the kitchen bench while cooking. Constraints: zero cost, sits beside the toaster, paper or cardboard from home. One checkable criterion: holds 10 cards and they stay put when the bench is wiped once, with no sharp edge. This worked transfer is not Jordan’s organiser and it is not a puzzle-piece brief. Safety stays the same: scissors only, adult nearby, no power tool.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe friend loses hair ties in a sports bag; one constraint is home cardboard that fits the side pocket. User: the friend. Problem: hair ties vanish in the bag. Criterion: holds 6 hair ties and they stay in when the bag is tipped gently once, with no sharp edge. That is the same process with new facts. “Holds 8 pencils” does not describe hair ties. Scissors and an adult nearby remain the making limit when a model is eventually planned.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Copying Jordan’s “holds 8 pencils” criterion onto a new user, and changing only the title. The process stays, but the facts must change. A new person has a new need, new constraints and new checks. If the criterion still talks about pencils, the transfer has not happened. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about transfer?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTransfer means using the same design process for a new user and a new need, not copying Jordan’s pencil answers. The process is: name the user and the problem, write a brief with constraints, set checks you can try, describe real objects you can see, sketch different structures, choose one and name a trade-off, plan cardboard and scissors with an adult nearby, separate the prototype from the final idea, test, record a fail as information, and evaluate what was met. A new user changes the facts. “Holds 8 pencils” is Jordan’s criterion. It does not belong on someone else’s brief unless that person also has that need. Copying the title and keeping every pencil sentence is not transfer. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 12: The design process","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"The design process","week":9,"week_in_unit":6,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA grandparent’s recipe cards slide off the bench; the process moves to that new user without pencil criteria. User and problem: a grandparent, recipe cards slide off the kitchen bench while cooking. Constraints: zero cost, sits beside the toaster, paper or cardboard from home. One checkable criterion: holds 10 cards and they stay put when the bench is wiped once, with no sharp edge. This worked transfer is not Jordan’s organiser and it is not a puzzle-piece brief. Safety stays the same: scissors only, adult nearby, no power tool.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"The design-process steps beside a reminder that a new user needs new facts.","caption":"Transfer keeps the process and changes the user, need and criteria.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Same process</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">User, limits, check, test</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">New facts</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Not the pencil answers</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A recipe-card worked example beside the separate sibling puzzle-piece brief.","caption":"The independent brief uses a younger sibling, not Jordan’s pencils.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Recipe-card model</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">10 cards stay when wiped</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sibling task</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Puzzle pieces on the floor</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-design-technology-y9-w06-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
